## Series context & the key point _(why this concept, not a generic one)_

Part 3.1 is the **second "Qualities" chapter** and the explosive end of the spectrum. Part 3.0 split *endurance* into central/local/work-capacity; Part 3.1 takes the three "athletic-looking" qualities — **power, speed, agility** — and proves they are all the *same physics problem*: producing force **fast**, not producing the **most** force. That one distinction reorganizes everything that follows (why explosive work looks nothing like strength work, why it's quality not conditioning, why it gates on a strength base, why it gets you hurt).

The four objects the reader must come away with:

1. **The controlling distinction — RATE, not PEAK.** Real movement gives you only **~0.1–0.3 seconds** of ground contact — a jump, a cut, a sprint stride. Reaching your *maximum* force takes longer than that. So the athlete who pours out the most force in those first fractions of a second wins, even with a smaller 1RM. The trained variable is **Rate of Force Development (RFD) — the *steepness* of the force-vs-time curve, not its height.** This is the single non-obvious idea the whole poster must make visible.
2. **Three flavours of "fast" — one engine, three outputs.** Power (explosive force in place: jump, throw, clean — *force × velocity*), speed (that power applied to locomotion: acceleration vs max-velocity, two separate skills), agility (speed + the ability to *stop, change direction, react*). Same phosphagen engine + Type IIx fibres; distinct outputs, distinct protocols.
3. **The elastic engine — the stretch-shortening cycle (SSC).** Rapid stretch → stores elastic energy + triggers a stretch reflex → snaps back into the next contraction (a stretched rubber band). The catch is the **amortization phase** — the split-second between stretch and push-off; the *shorter* it is, the more elastic energy you reclaim. **RSI = jump height ÷ ground-contact time** reads whether you're *elastic-dominant* (fast rebound) or *strength-dominant* (force from a dead stop but you leak the bounce) — and that tells you which tool you need.
4. **It all rests on strength — the force-velocity ceiling.** Power is just strength expressed quickly; *you cannot express force you don't have.* Max strength raises the **whole** force-velocity curve (more force at every speed); explosive work then shifts expression toward the velocity end. Strength + plyo beats either alone. And the entry ticket is literal: **~1.5× bodyweight squat + 30 s single-leg balance + 30 s single-leg half-squat** gates *high-intensity* plyometrics; low-intensity plyos start earlier.

The protocols fall straight out of "rate, not peak" — all three are **fresh-state quality work**: low reps, maximal intent, full rest, stop the instant speed drops.

- **Power:** light–moderate load (BW → ~30–60% 1RM), **1–5 reps**, **2–3 min rest**, 2–3×/wk, done fresh. Toolkit ladder: ballistic lifts → KB swings → plyo progression (low→mod→high) → Oly variations (if coached) → contrast/complex (PAP: heavy primer then explosive).
- **Speed = two skills, trained separately:** acceleration (10–30 m, sled/hill, forward lean, long drive) vs maximum velocity (flying sprints, upright, short "punch-down" contacts). Low volume, full rest, fresh.
- **Agility = brakes first:** deceleration (the real limiter on change-of-direction speed) → pre-planned COD (cone drills, 5-10-5) → reactive (respond to a stimulus). *Reactive* is the only true agility; the 5-10-5 is honestly a *change-of-direction* test, not an agility test.

The mechanism turn ("now the physics") wants instruments, not prose: **RFD** (slope of the force-time curve, contact window 0.1–0.3 s), the **force-velocity curve** (the tradeoff + the trainable ceiling), **motor units / neural drive** (Type IIx recruitment + rate coding; early gains are neural), the **SSC + amortization**, and **RSI**.

Prior Fit posters each used a distinct sub-archetype inside the locked style: lookup matrices (1.1/1.2), a signal-trace day strip (2.0 Aesthetic), an operating-band curve dashboard (3.1 Aesthetic), a build-up assembly schematic (3.2 Aesthetic), a dual-config datasheet (3.3 Aesthetic), a P&ID oxygen-flow diagram (2.1 Athletic), a cutaway powertrain (1.0 Athletic), a vertical capacity column (2.0 Athletic), and a two-channel fault-isolation bench (3.0 Athletic). Part 3.1's native geometry is a **force measured against time in milliseconds** — so the right new instrument is an **oscilloscope / signal-tuning bench**: a force-**time** trace where the hero argument *is* a slope, paired with a force-**velocity** tuning chart. Nobody has used a millisecond-scale oscilloscope yet, and it's the most honest possible rendering of "rate, not peak."

**The single key point to make a reader feel:** *Power, speed, and agility aren't about how much force you can make — they're about how* fast *you can turn it on. You get ~0.1–0.3 seconds of ground contact; in that window the* steep *force curve beats the* tall *one. So you train the slope, not the peak — low reps, full intent, full rest, always fresh — and you build the elastic rebound (short amortization, high RSI). But you can only express force you already own: a 1.5× bodyweight squat is the gate, and strength is the ceiling everything else is shifted along.*

