## 1. The Big Idea

_The three "fast" qualities are one physics problem — **rate of force development, the steepness of the force-time curve, not its peak** — because real movement only gives you ~0.1–0.3 s of ground contact. The poster is an oscilloscope / signal-tuning bench an engineer would mount on a wall: a hero **force-vs-time trace** where an explosive curve (steep, earlier) out-produces a strength curve (tall, lazy) *inside the shaded contact window* even though the strength curve peaks higher; a paired **force-velocity tuning chart** showing the trainable ceiling; the **three flavours of fast** as three tuned output channels each with its spec; the **stretch-shortening cycle** as a three-beat timing diagram with an RSI gauge; and a hard **safety interlock** (the 1.5× BW squat gate) controlling access to high-intensity work._

## 2. Visual Metaphor & Structure

An **oscilloscope / signal-tuning bench** — the instrument an engineer uses to read a *fast* signal. The whole poster turns on one move the medium can make that prose can't: **drawing a slope and shading a time window so the reader literally sees the steep curve beat the tall one where it counts.**

**HERO — the RFD oscilloscope (force vs time).**
A graph with **FORCE** on the y-axis and **TIME in milliseconds** on the x-axis (label the axis `0 — 100 — 200 — 300 ms …` so the scale reads as *milliseconds*, the article's whole point). Two traces rise from the origin:

- **STRENGTH trace** — climbs to a **high peak** but **lazily**; it crosses its max *after* ~300–400 ms.
- **EXPLOSIVE / POWER trace** — a **much steeper** initial rise that peaks **earlier and lower**.

A **shaded vertical band marks the CONTACT WINDOW (0.1–0.3 s)** — the only time you actually have. *Inside that band the explosive trace sits ABOVE the strength trace.* One bold callout on the slope itself: `RFD = ΔForce ÷ Δtime = the SLOPE, not the peak.` A second, smaller: `you don't have time to reach max force — you have 0.1–0.3 s.` This single graphic carries RFD + the "fast not most" thesis + why explosive ≠ strength work, in one geometry.

**PAIRED INSTRUMENT — the force-velocity tuning chart.**
Beside/below the oscilloscope, the classic downward F-V curve (high force/low velocity → low force/high velocity). Mark the two training zones on it: `HEAVY / SLOW → force end` and `LIGHT / FAST → velocity end`. Then the key move: a **ghosted lower curve and a solid raised curve with an upward arrow** labelled `MAX STRENGTH raises the WHOLE curve` and `explosive work shifts you toward the velocity end →`. Caption: `you need the whole curve — that's why strength + plyo beats either alone.` This is the "strength relationship" rendered as geometry, not a paragraph.

**THREE OUTPUT CHANNELS — the three flavours of fast.**
A row of three tuned-output modules sharing one input rail labelled `one engine: phosphagen system + Type IIx fibres`:

- **POWER** — glyph: explosive force in place (a force vector off the ground). Spec chip: `BW–60% 1RM · 1–5 reps · 2–3 min rest · fresh`. Toolkit ladder (accessible→advanced): `ballistic lifts · KB swings · plyo low→mod→high · Oly (if coached) · contrast/PAP`. Benchmark tag: `vertical & broad jump ↑`.
- **SPEED** — drawn as **two sub-tracks** (the article's "two skills"): `ACCELERATION (10–30 m · sled/hill · lean · long drive)` and `MAX VELOCITY (flying sprints · upright · short contacts)`. Spec: `low volume · full rest · fresh · 2–3×/wk`. Benchmark: `10 m time ↓ · flying 20–30 m ↓`.
- **AGILITY** — drawn as a **brakes-first 3-rung ladder** with a directional lock: `1 DECELERATE (the real limiter) → 2 PRE-PLANNED COD (cones · 5-10-5) → 3 REACTIVE (respond to a stimulus)`. A small fault chip: `5-10-5 is a CHANGE-OF-DIRECTION test, not agility — no reaction.` Benchmark: `5-10-5 ↓ · reactive cleaner`. Rule chip: `don't skip to reactive before you can stop & cut clean.`

**THE ELASTIC ENGINE — SSC timing diagram + RSI gauge.**
A small three-beat timing strip (rubber-band logic): `STRETCH (load · store elastic energy + stretch reflex) → ⟨AMORTIZATION — keep it SHORT⟩ → SHORTEN (push off · release)`. The amortization beat is drawn as a *narrow gap* with the note `shorter gap = more elastic energy reclaimed`. Next to it, an **RSI gauge** — a dial reading between two poles: `STRENGTH-DOMINANT (force from a dead stop, leaks the bounce) ↔ ELASTIC-DOMINANT (fast rebound)`, formula stamped `RSI = jump height ÷ ground-contact time`, caption `tells you which tool you need.` A quiet neural note pinned nearby: `early gains are NEURAL — recruiting Type IIx + firing them earlier/faster (rate coding), not new tissue.`

**THE SAFETY INTERLOCK — the prerequisite gate.**
A literal engineering **interlock / gate** controlling the high-intensity path: inputs `back squat ~1.5× BW · single-leg balance 30 s · single-leg half-squat 30 s` must all be satisfied to open the gate to `HIGH-INTENSITY PLYOS (depth jumps · max sprints)`. A bypass lane shows `LOW-INTENSITY PLYOS (pogos · squat jumps · low box jumps)` pass *freely* — `you can start here today`. Warning stamp: `several × bodyweight through joints in a fraction of a second — earn the right.` This is the one place the **danger/warning state** earns its keep (the chapter with the highest injury ceiling).

**FOOTER — the scoreboard + the law.**
A thin tracking strip of check-readouts: `vertical ↑ · broad jump ↑ · 10 m ↓ · flying 20–30 m ↓ · 5-10-5 ↓ · RSI ↑`, header `THE NUMBER IS THE BOSS`. A single closing law line, set apart: `POWER = STRENGTH EXPRESSED FAST — you can't express force you don't have.` Plus the diagnostic branch as a one-liner: `strength climbing but jumps/sprints flat? → you have force, not RATE — add full-intent explosive work.`

This sub-archetype — **a millisecond-scale force-time oscilloscope + a force-velocity tuning chart, feeding three tuned output channels, with an SSC timing diagram, an RSI gauge, and a hard safety interlock** — is unmistakably "Engineering Diagnostic Instrument" yet distinct from every prior Fit poster (no prior poster has plotted force against *time in milliseconds*, and none has used a safety interlock).

## 3. Layout — exact composition

**Orientation: portrait** (poster / blog-hero friendly, ~3:4 or 4:5). Eye path: the hero oscilloscope grabs first, then down through the three channels, the elastic engine, the interlock, and the footer law.

- **Top band (~12%) — HEADLINE + the thesis.** Headline lockup **"RATE, NOT PEAK."** sub: *power, speed, agility — how fast you turn force on, not how much you have.* A tiny axis hint (a ms tick mark) ties the headline to the hero.
- **Upper band (~26%) — HERO OSCILLOSCOPE.** The force-vs-time graph with the two traces and the shaded 0.1–0.3 s contact window. Largest, brightest object on the poster — the explosive trace winning inside the window is the visual punchline. Carries ~55% of the upper visual weight.
- **Right of / below the hero (~14%) — F-V TUNING CHART.** The downward force-velocity curve with the two zones + the raised-ceiling ghost/solid pair. Reads as the hero's paired instrument (same "physics bench").
- **Middle band (~22%) — THREE OUTPUT CHANNELS** side by side (POWER · SPEED · AGILITY), sharing the `one engine` input rail across their tops. Each is a compact spec card with its glyph, prescription chip, and benchmark tag. Speed shows its two sub-tracks; agility shows its 3-rung brakes-first ladder.
- **Lower-middle band (~14%) — THE ELASTIC ENGINE.** SSC three-beat timing diagram on the left, RSI gauge on the right, neural note pinned between.
- **Lower band (~8%) — THE SAFETY INTERLOCK.** The prerequisite gate with its bypass lane for low-intensity plyos. Visually weightier/sterner than the rest (warning treatment) so it reads as a *control*, not a footnote.
- **Footer (~6%) — the tracking strip + the closing law line.**

Visual hierarchy, largest→smallest: (1) the oscilloscope (steep beats tall inside the window), (2) the F-V tuning chart, (3) the three output channels, (4) the SSC timing + RSI, (5) the safety interlock, (6) the tracking strip + law.

